<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cao Pi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">187–226 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cao Pi was the son of the warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a> and the first emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-wei">Wei dynasty</a>. In 220 CE he deposed the last Han emperor and declared his own dynasty. The act formally ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty</a> and opened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-kingdoms-period">Three Kingdoms period</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cao-pi.jpg" alt="Cao Pi" width="306" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yan Li-pen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cao_Pi_Tang.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The dynasty ended in 220 CE when his son <b>Cao Pi</b> deposed Emperor Xian and declared the Wei dynasty, opening the Three Kingdoms period before China was briefly reunified under the Jin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Emperor Xian, the last emperor of the Eastern Han dynasty and long a puppet of warlords, was forced to abdicate by <b>Cao Pi</b>, son of Cao Cao.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-11">11 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After his death in early 220 his son <b>Cao Pi</b> forced Emperor Xian to abdicate and proclaimed the Wei dynasty, with its capital at Luoyang.</blockquote><a href="/state/cao-wei">Cao Wei</a></li><li><blockquote>After <b>Cao Pi</b> ended the Han in 220, Liu Bei proclaimed himself emperor at Chengdu in 221, presenting his state as the legitimate Han.</blockquote><a href="/state/shu-han">Shu Han</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Bozhou, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.86,115.77&amp;year=212" aria-label="Bozhou on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7502 936 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Cao Pi"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7952 1206h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7952 1206h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.86,115.77&amp;year=212">What was here in 212 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Alas that I was born in these times, To be blown along like a cloud puffed by the wind!&#8221;</p><footer>He abandons the campaign &#183; tr. Arthur Waley, 1918 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/42290/pg42290-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;To sit idle is not my desire: Gladly enough would I go to my country’s aid.&#8221;</p><footer>The campaign against Wu &#183; tr. Arthur Waley, 1918 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/42290/pg42290-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
